te D, compare with B, and maybe adjust recommendation if D looks better. Let's review notes for D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper, weak leadership, loner. Concerns: know-it-all attitude, hot temper, weak leadership, loner (crew cooperation). Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration, handles unexpected events. No explicit stress resilience, but responds adequately to unexpected events. Could be okay. But crew cooperation concerns are significant.\n\nWe could argue that D's hot temper and loner attitude likely make crew cooperation problematic, perhaps worse than B's grumpy/uncooperative. Also D's weak leadership is a concern for long-haul. So B still likely best.\n\nWe could also ask for more info about D's crew cooperation, but we have no tool left? We have tools to ask Sarah or Emily. We could ask Sarah for observations on D's crew cooperation. But we have already used Sarah's turn. We could ask Emily for D's crew cooperation. But we might want to keep discussion moving.\n\nGiven the instruction: we should not ask new questions without need. We have enough info to argue that D's concerns outweigh strengths. So we can keep vote B, argue that D's hot temper and loner attitude are serious crew cooperation concerns, making B still preferable despite his communication issues.\n\nThus James's public message: I agree with Emily that B remains best, note D's issues, and suggest we still need to discuss D's crew cooperation but likely B remains.\n\nLet's craft.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
